  "request": {
    "spec": "Redesign the identity key: identityKey = SHA-256 hex over (normalizedTelephone + '|' + lowerEmail + '|' + soundex(lastName)); duplicate detection (409) uses this key, still ignoring owners flagged deleted and still applying the email-domain blocklist first. This changes existing behaviour: the soft-match must now trigger when the identityKey differs but soundex(lastName) and postcode match. Because the telephone is part of the key, two owners with the same lastName and postcode but DIFFERENT telephones are no longer a hard household duplicate: they are created as a soft match (not rejected with 409). Duplicate detection is now the single identity key, so the separate household-duplicate 409 block from the computed householdId no longer applies.",
    "prompt": "Implement the following change to the application so that it fully satisfies the\nspecification and all existing and new tests pass.\n\nWrite it to keep structural impact low. Structural impact is a measurable cost, not a\nvague goal. For every function you change, the cost is:\n    cost = max(WMC_other, 1) * CC * max(1, changed_lines)\nCC is that function's cyclomatic complexity. WMC_other is the summed cyclomatic\ncomplexity of the OTHER methods in its class, which is the surrounding context you must\nhold to edit it safely. changed_lines is how many of its lines you add or edit. The costs\nare summed over every function you change, then multiplied by the number of files you\ntouch. So the biggest lever is WMC_other: a method inside a large class pays for the whole\nclass. Do not grow one method or one class. Add behaviour as small, cohesive units, each\nwith little surrounding complexity, and keep every function you change simple.\n\nRun the test suite and make it green before finishing.\n\nRedesign the identity key: identityKey = SHA-256 hex over (normalizedTelephone + '|' + lowerEmail + '|' + soundex(lastName)); duplicate detection (409) uses this key, still ignoring owners flagged deleted and still applying the email-domain blocklist first. This changes existing behaviour: the soft-match must now trigger when the identityKey differs but soundex(lastName) and postcode match. Because the telephone is part of the key, two owners with the same lastName and postcode but DIFFERENT telephones are no longer a hard household duplicate: they are created as a soft match (not rejected with 409). Duplicate detection is now the single identity key, so the separate household-duplicate 409 block from the computed householdId no longer applies.\n"
  },
